{{- /* Copyright (c) 2026 Michael D Henderson. */ -}}
{{define "content"}}
<h1>Invitations</h1>
<p class="sub">Registration is invite-only: there is no sign-up form. An invitation is good for 48 hours and for one use, and the link is shown once &mdash; what this system stores is a hash of it, so there is nothing to show you a second time.</p>
{{with .Problem}}<p class="problem">{{.}}</p>{{end}}

{{with .Created}}
<div class="card">
  <h2>Send this link to {{.Invitation.Email}}</h2>
  <p class="sub">This is the only time it is shown. It stops working at {{stamp .Invitation.ExpiresAt}} UTC, or the moment it is used, or the moment you invite this address again.</p>
  <p class="row">
    <input id="invitation-link" type="text" readonly value="{{.Link}}" style="flex:1" onfocus="this.select()" {{noAutofill}}>
    <button type="button" class="primary" data-copy="invitation-link">Copy</button>
  </p>
  <p class="muted">There is no e-mail transport yet, so sending it is yours to do. With JavaScript off, the box above selects itself when you click it.</p>
</div>
{{- /* The copy affordance, and the whole of the JavaScript on this page. The
       link is 43 characters of base64 after an origin, and expecting somebody
       to select it by hand without clipping the first character is how an
       invitation gets reported as broken. With scripting off the button does
       nothing and the input still selects itself on focus, which is why the
       input is there rather than a <code> element. */ -}}
<script>
document.querySelectorAll("button[data-copy]").forEach(function (button) {
  button.addEventListener("click", function () {
    var field = document.getElementById(button.getAttribute("data-copy"));
    if (!field) { return; }
    field.select();
    if (navigator.clipboard) {
      navigator.clipboard.writeText(field.value).then(function () {
        button.textContent = "Copied";
      }, function () {
        button.textContent = "Press \u2318C";
      });
      return;
    }
    button.textContent = "Press \u2318C";
  });
});
</script>
{{end}}

<div class="card">
  <p class="row">
    <a class="button" href="/admin/invitations">Pending</a>
    <a class="button" href="/admin/invitations?status=all">Everything</a>
    {{range .Statuses}}<a class="button" href="/admin/invitations?status={{.}}">{{.}}</a>{{end}}
  </p>
  <p class="muted">Showing: {{.Filter}}. No invitation is ever deleted &mdash; a terminal one stays so that &ldquo;who invited this person, and did they ever accept&rdquo; keeps an answer.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Status</th><th>Expires</th><th>Invited by</th><th>Account</th><th>Reason</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {{range .Invitations}}
        <tr>
          <td>{{.Email}}</td>
          <td>
            <span class="pill">{{.Status}}</span>
            {{if and (eq .Status "pending") (.Expired $.Now)}}<span class="pill">expired</span>{{end}}
          </td>
          <td>{{stamp .ExpiresAt}}</td>
          <td>{{with .InvitedByName}}{{.}}{{else}}&mdash;{{end}}</td>
          <td class="mono">{{with .UserUID}}{{.}}{{else}}&mdash;{{end}}</td>
          <td>{{with .Reason}}{{.}}{{else}}&mdash;{{end}}</td>
          <td>
            {{if eq .Status "pending"}}
              <form class="inline-form" method="post" action="/admin/invitations/{{.UID}}/revoke">
                <input type="text" name="reason" placeholder="why (optional)" {{noAutofill}}>
                <button type="submit">Revoke</button>
              </form>
            {{end}}
          </td>
        </tr>
      {{else}}
        <tr><td colspan="7" class="empty">nothing to show</td></tr>
      {{end}}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Invite somebody</h2>
  <p class="sub">Inviting an address that already has a pending invitation replaces it: the new link works and the old one stops. That is also how a lapsed invitation is replaced &mdash; there is deliberately no way to extend one, renew an expired one, or force one to expire, because an administrator cannot see the link they would be extending.</p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/invitations">
    <label for="email">Email address</label>
    {{- /* type="text" with inputmode="email", not type="email" (invariant 23).
           The pair type="email" + name="email" is the exact signature a password
           manager reads as "your username", and this box holds the address of
           somebody who does not have an account yet -- the one value it can
           never usefully carry is the address of the person typing. inputmode
           keeps the "@" on a phone keyboard; domain.ValidateEmail is the check,
           as it always was, because a check in the browser is not a check. */ -}}
    <input id="email" type="text" inputmode="email" name="email" required {{noAutofill}}>
    <p><button class="primary" type="submit">Create the invitation</button></p>
  </form>
</div>
{{end}}
